{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1336 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d54dd2e682daa0d93b3a0159b1bccabbf1a6cb1da0e1fcbd2f3487c4e62f7f22" :edb/db-before-t 1335 :edb/db-after-t 1336 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534648 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:09:51.832Z" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192256 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192256 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534648 false] [17592186192256 1145 "First, we substitute $u=x^4$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{1}^{a}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{2^{-u}}{\\ln 2}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[2^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2^x}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/769170756b4db689d820f57e3f8d9791.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= -\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (0 - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8\\ln 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 false] [17592186192256 1145 "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192257 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192257 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty e^{-3x} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534648 false] [17592186192257 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{∞}e^{-3x}dx &= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1}^{a}e^{-3x}dx \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{e^{-3x}}{3}]_{-1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[e^{-3x}]_{-1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)e^{-3a} - e^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/121e8b397fd3d1ba5e806dfe5ca38245.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{∞}e^{-3x} &= -\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)e^{-3a} - e^{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (0 - e^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 false] [17592186192257 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192292 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192292 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} 3\\cdot 2^{2x+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534648 false] [17592186192292 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}3 \\cdot 2^{2x + 1}dx &= 3 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)∫_{a}^{0}2^{2x + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[(2^{2x + 1})/(2\\ln (2))]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot 2^{1})/(2\\ln (2)) \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[2^{2x}]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)(2^{0} - 2^{2a}) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - lim_(a → - ∞)2^{2a})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/e9da045e68ed322bd54138b5cd02b995.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}3 \\cdot 2^{2x + 1}dx &= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - lim_(a → - ∞)2^{2a}) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 false] [17592186192292 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186192541 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301304 13194139534648 true] [17592186192541 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301356 13194139534648 true] [17592186192541 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301359 13194139534648 true] [17592186192541 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301363 13194139534648 true] [17592186192542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301325 13194139534648 true] [17592186192542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301339 13194139534648 true] [17592186192542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301343 13194139534648 true] [17592186192542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301346 13194139534648 true] [17592186192542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301350 13194139534648 true] [17592186192572 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301297 13194139534648 true] [17592186192572 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301311 13194139534648 true] [17592186192572 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301318 13194139534648 true] [17592186192572 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301332 13194139534648 true] [17592186192572 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301353 13194139534648 true] [17592186193070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534648 true] [17592186193070 1100 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186193070 1100 "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}dx$." 13194139534648 false] [17592186193070 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/5e9932629e5a201c2ada61157fdfa39351962a55116e2980157b1823907159f2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301297 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301298 13194139534648 true] [17592186301297 1089 "q-152230" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301297 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534648 true] [17592186301297 1100 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301297 1113 #uuid "3c351560-bc86-5c54-b119-540a5f2a9855" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301297 1145 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{1}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301299 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301300 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301301 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301302 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301303 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1041 "selection" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301302 13194139534648 true] [17592186301298 1110 #uuid "02a401f7-41c4-5faa-99ac-1eac8afda80a" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301299 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534648 true] [17592186301299 1111 #uuid "1fb9c396-827e-5b37-a80b-769aab9304e8" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301300 1034 "The integral diverges" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534648 true] [17592186301300 1111 #uuid "2aef1160-a55c-54