me/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/2fc15149d2097a1965e575e725b4dfb49401c1fe5bb1c5b3e43bf0f4f51743a1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301355 13194139534648 true] [17592186301354 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534648 true] [17592186301354 1041 "field-1" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301354 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301355 13194139534648 true] [17592186301354 1110 #uuid "6a136a6e-5687-5056-8d3d-14542c6260d6" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301355 1034 "\\frac{5}{\\ln(5)}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534648 true] [17592186301355 1111 #uuid "ba89a20e-ecec-5f2f-974d-efb594302db0" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301356 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301357 13194139534648 true] [17592186301356 1089 "q-351513" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301356 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534648 true] [17592186301356 1100 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301356 1113 #uuid "b7dd0fc4-1e7b-59a0-83e5-4685362a10c6" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301356 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301358 13194139534648 true] [17592186301357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534648 true] [17592186301357 1041 "field-1" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301358 13194139534648 true] [17592186301357 1110 #uuid "9c2c721b-d336-528e-8596-b9b78c5776b2" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301358 1034 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534648 true] [17592186301358 1111 #uuid "5d2b1a32-5ab9-588d-80de-b21ffe231aff" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301360 13194139534648 true] [17592186301359 1089 "q-351523" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534648 true] [17592186301359 1100 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301359 1113 #uuid "680b7856-af9d-5109-9fd9-00152a4dd785" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301359 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301361 13194139534648 true] [17592186301360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301362 13194139534648 true] [17592186301360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534648 true] [17592186301360 1041 "field-1" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301361 13194139534648 true] [17592186301360 1110 #uuid "ad63441b-0d19-55c1-8d5b-d23447518684" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301361 1034 "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534648 true] [17592186301361 1111 #uuid "37cacb50-acde-5f4c-98ec-48ef18e314f9" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301362 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534648 true] [17592186301362 1111 #uuid "81bf3d4b-df09-53f0-9589-1ccd022fa30e" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301363 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301364 13194139534648 true] [17592186301363 1089 "q-351539" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534648 true] [17592186301363 1100 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301363 1113 #uuid "5e754189-9978-5a00-8148-3768cbdf2063" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301363 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u-1}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{3a-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x-1}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/6a5fd63a29d7c8f5fba712c7fa6c20620c9a1438750f3197606e726a85666827.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534648 true] [17592186301364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301365 13194139534648 true] [17592186301364 1040 #edb/ref 